From b62070f835f9489c9ecd74adc3f9497f98da7690 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:03:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(runner-local): recover running tasks left with no lease
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`expireStaleLeases` is the only evidence that a process died mid-attempt, and
it can only report a lease that still EXISTS. That leaves a hole the runner's
own comments already describe: a recovery pass expires a lease, ends the
attempt, and dies before re-dispatching. The task stays `running` with no lease
— invisible to every later expiry sweep, because the row it would have been
found by is already deleted. Its chat stays busy and cancellation has nothing
local to abort. The only record was `pendingRedispatch`, an in-memory Set that
the crash takes with it.

Adds `TaskStore.selectOrphanedRunningTasks(limit)`: running tasks holding no
lease, oldest first, bounded. It is a QUERY over existing rows and needs no
schema change — deliberate, because the SQLite adapter refuses to open a
database whose schema version differs and ships no migrations, so a recovery fix
that bumped the version would strand every existing user's conversations.

The runner sweeps orphans on a timer (default: half the lease TTL) and feeds
them through the existing `pendingRedispatch` path, so they take the same tested
route as a parked task: status re-checked, attempt budget honoured, and
`startAttempt` acquiring a fresh lease — that acquisition, not a flag, is what
arbitrates two recoverers reaching one task.

The periodic pass deliberately does NOT expire leases. `expireStaleLeases`
deletes rows, and on a timer it cannot tell "the owner died" from "that owner is
busy and its heartbeat was late". With two runners over one store an IDLE one
would strip a BUSY one of the lease it needs to commit its own result — strictly
worse than not sweeping, because nothing recovers it either. The existing test
"does not steal the task back when the lease moved during the backoff" is that
exact shape and is right to fail such a sweep; it caught this during
development. So expiry stays where a caller asserts a death, `recover()`, and
the timer does only the part that is always safe: tasks with no lease belong to
nobody by construction.

An embedder that also wants periodic expiry calls `recover()` on its own
schedule, which it can do safely precisely because it knows how many hosts it
runs. OneCAD does.

BREAKING: `TaskStore` gains a required method, and `RecoveryReport` gains
`orphaned`. Both in-tree adapters implement it and the conformance suite
requires it.

Tests: store conformance in both adapters (no-lease reported; live AND expired
leases excluded, since an expired lease is `expireStaleLeases`'s business;
queued excluded; limit honoured; non-positive limit returns nothing) and three
runner cases. Verified red-first: 2 of the 3 runner cases fail with the sweep
disabled. Full suite 1654 passed / 1 pre-existing failure
(`packages/client/tests/resume.test.ts`, which fails identically on the
untouched pin).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
